  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe storms expected this afternoon\nand evening with damaging winds and flash flooding potential. \n\nExpect one more afternoon and evening of strong to severe\nthunderstorms before a cold front pushes through and off the\neastern seaboard sometime Tuesday morning. This will bring a \ncouple days of dry weather to the region before cyclonic flow \naloft builds back in for Thursday through the weekend, bringing \nmore chances for rain. \n \nA MCV embedded in westerly flow aloft was evident on satellite \nimagery over KY and will approach WV/VA this afternoon around \nthe time of peak heating. A cold front was also moving through \nthe OH and TN Valleys albeit slowly. Expect scattered to \nnumerous showers and thunderstorms to develop in SE WV and shift\nsoutheast throughout the afternoon and evening ahead of the \nfront. Damaging wind gusts will be the main concern in our \nregion, with a modestly unstable air mass in place. There should\nbe a negligible amount of turning in the lower atmosphere, so \ntornadoes are not expected. There is a theta-e/instability \ngradient near the VA/NC border where higher dew points were \npooling, along a lee trough. South of this area, most of the \nDeep South were under heat advisories; to the north, \ntemperatures were cooler. CAMs agree that parts of the VA and NC\nPiedmont and the western mountains may see the strongest storms\ntonight. \n\nAlong with the damaging wind threat, PWATs in the 1.7-2.1 inch \nrange will pose a localized flash flood threat where the \nheaviest storms develop and/or train. Expected instantaneous \nrainfall rates in the 3 to 6 inch per hour range will also \nsupport urban, small stream, and flash flooding. Could see some\nlighter showers in SE WV overnight with the actual cold frontal\npassage, which should begin to push through around midnight.",
